    "date": "January 26, 1624",
    "place": "Naples",
    "archival": "ARSI, FG 737, 94",
    "excerpt": "It is already three years since His Divine Majesty deigned to give me the vocation to enter the Society, and from that time He also gave me another, of no lesser worth — that of going to the Indies, to convert those souls redeemed with the blood of Christ our Lord; driven by which I come to supplicate Your Paternity, for love of that same Christ, to will to put this my desire into effect. I write you nothing more, for I know that for your great charity you will do me this grace.",
    "kind": "abridged",
    "full_paras": [
     "It is already three years since His Divine Majesty deigned to give me the vocation to enter the Society, and from that time He also gave me another, of no lesser worth — that of going to the Indies, to convert those souls redeemed with the blood of Christ our Lord; driven by which I come to supplicate Your Paternity, for love of that same Christ, to will to put this my desire into effect. I write you nothing more, for I know that for your great charity you will do me this grace."
    ]
   },
   {
    "n": 69,
    "year": 1628,
    "date": "September 9, 1628",
    "place": "Naples",
    "archival": "ARSI, FG 738, 274",
    "excerpt": "A long time ago I wrote to Your Paternity to remember me on occasions of sending men to the New World, to toil in something for the conversion of those peoples; I return now with this to remind you of the same, to manifest to you anew my desires in this regard, which grow greater in me day by day. Fulfill them for love of God at last, Your Paternity, and do not look upon my little ability and my little fervor, which seem to render me unworthy of so many graces; for I too hope — trusting, however, in the strength of my Lord — to be able to bow my neck to naked swords and open my breast to lances when need be, as so many of His sons continually do with so much glory.",
    "kind": "abridged",
    "full_paras": [
     "A long time ago I wrote to Your Paternity to remember me on occasions of sending men to the New World, to toil in something for the conversion of those peoples; I return now with this to remind you of the same, to manifest to you anew my desires in this regard, which grow greater in me day by day. Fulfill them for love of God at last, Your Paternity, and do not look upon my little ability and my little fervor, which seem to render me unworthy of so many graces; for I too hope — trusting, however, in the strength of my Lord — to be able to bow my neck to naked swords and open my breast to lances when need be, as so many of His sons continually do with so much glory.",
     "Second my hopes, Your Paternity, and do not leave me idle in Italy, where to my great shame so many departures of our Fathers are seen every day; nor show yourself partial (pardon the affection if I use such a word), remembering others and not me. I would write in blood, that it might bear witness to what I desire and be fulfilled the sooner; but I trust in the charity with which Your Paternity is wont ever to console his sons, that you will not forget me. I stand awaiting the notice as the final completion and most desired end to which all my labors are directed. And finally, in your holy sacrifices before His Divine Majesty, do not forget me, who have so great need."
    ]
   },
   {
    "n": 90,
    "year": 1637,
    "date": "October 9, 1637",
    "place": "St. [illegible]",
    "archival": "ARSI, FG 741, 47",
    "excerpt": "It is many years since I wrote to Your Paternity, asking you as a grace to destine me to the mission of the East Indies; but partly from finding myself in the midst of my course of studies, and partly from my sins, was I the cause that I did not merit so signal a favor. Now finding myself at the end of my theology, no longer encumbered with studies, I return to remind Your Paternity of it, the more so since it is said that the procurator of those parts is shortly to come here. I therefore cast myself at your feet, and most humbly pray you, with all the affection I can and with all the force I know how, that you will make me set sail for the Indies as soon as may be; and I ask you this grace for all you love the salvation of my soul, for all you desire that I make satisfaction in some part for the infinite multitude of my sins, in visceribus Iesu Christi (in the heart of Jesus Christ).",
    "kind": "abridged",
    "full_paras": [
     "It is many years since I wrote to Your Paternity, asking you as a grace to destine me to the mission of the East Indies; but partly from finding myself in the midst of my course of studies, and partly from my sins, was I the cause that I did not merit so signal a favor. Now finding myself at the end of my theology, no longer encumbered with studies, I return to remind Your Paternity of it, the more so since it is said that the procurator of those parts is shortly to come here. I therefore cast myself at your feet, and most humbly pray you, with all the affection I can and with all the force I know how, that you will make me set sail for the Indies as soon as may be; and I ask you this grace for all you love the salvation of my soul, for all you desire that I make satisfaction in some part for the infinite multitude of my sins, in visceribus Iesu Christi (in the heart of Jesus Christ). I shall never cease to importune Your Paternity with letters, and His Divine Majesty with prayers, until I obtain the going thither; but the desire you have for my salvation, I believe, will move you, though I do not merit it, to do me this grace — the greatest I can desire in this life. Wherewith, praying that you may have infinite years of life from heaven for the good of the Company and of the whole world, I end."
    ]
   },
   {
    "n": 92,
    "year": 1638,
    "date": "January 16, 1638",
    "place": "Naples",
    "archival": "ARSI, FG 741, 79",
    "excerpt": "The great desire, which Our Lord in His infinite mercy deigns to communicate to me, to go to the Indies, compels me to trouble Your Paternity with this letter. I pray you, therefore, with the greatest warmth I know and can, to grant me this favor, which, after my vocation, I esteem the greatest that can be granted me in this life; and I take the boldness to make you a fresh plea because, as I wrote you last time, this being the last year of my studies, I find myself already clear for the voyage.",
    "kind": "abridged",
    "full_paras": [
     "The great desire, which Our Lord in His infinite mercy deigns to communicate to me, to go to the Indies, compels me to trouble Your Paternity with this letter. I pray you, therefore, with the greatest warmth I know and can, to grant me this favor, which, after my vocation, I esteem the greatest that can be granted me in this life; and I take the boldness to make you a fresh plea because, as I wrote you last time, this being the last year of my studies, I find myself already clear for the voyage. I know how little worthy I am of this favor, but for all that I hope in the blood of Our Lord Jesus Christ, and in the kindness of Your Paternity, that you will deign to oblige me, to give me occasion to make satisfaction for my past failings, which, the greater they are, the greater my obligation to do something in the service of God. Meanwhile I am praying that Heaven grant you infinite years of life for the benefit of the Company, and I pray you to grant me the favor of your blessing, which I would have come together with the patent for the voyage."
    ]
   },
   {
    "n": 96,
    "year": 1638,
    "date": "June 19, 1638",
    "place": "Naples",
    "archival": "ARSI, FG 741, 138",
    "excerpt": "I believe this letter of mine will have the fortune of finding Your Paternity in Rome, and I rejoice that it comes into your hands together with the others, so that they may strike a greater blow and obtain from Your Paternity what I hope has already been obtained from Heaven. I dare to say this because night and day I do nothing but penances and prayers to this end, and not only I, but countless others make prayers and penances for me, to move first God and then the heart of Your Paternity to place me among the number of the elect and assign me to the Indies.",
    "kind": "abridged",
    "full_paras": [
     "I believe this letter of mine will have the fortune of finding Your Paternity in Rome, and I rejoice that it comes into your hands together with the others, so that they may strike a greater blow and obtain from Your Paternity what I hope has already been obtained from Heaven. I dare to say this because night and day I do nothing but penances and prayers to this end, and not only I, but countless others make prayers and penances for me, to move first God and then the heart of Your Paternity to place me among the number of the elect and assign me to the Indies. Oh, that I could reveal in some way the fire I feel in my heart, and the desire that God communicates to me! Reckon, Your Paternity, that there is no moment when I do not run to the Indies in thought; no word ever leaves my mouth that does not treat of those lands; I never close my eyes without dreaming of Japan, or the Ocean, or martyrdoms. In short, God has lifted me out of myself and out of Europe, and makes me dwell only in Asia among barbarians and infidels. Let Your Paternity make me follow God, who will have me at all costs, and be moved by those tears which I cannot show you, but which I shed continually for this grace before Saint Francis Xavier. I await the blessing of Your Paternity, which, though by no merit of mine deserved, is owed to my continual weeping, to prayer, to my most ardent desire, to the vow made to God with so much feeling, with the counsel of many holy men, with the leave of my Superiors, and lastly to the kindness of Your Paternity, from which I dare promise myself everything — all the more since going to the Indies is Your particular sentiment, as is read in Your letters. What use is it to spur us on, if then You would put the bridle on us just as we are about to run? With which I commend myself to the Holy Sacrifices and prayers of Your Paternity."
    ]
   },
   {
    "n": 98,
    "year": 1638,
    "date": "November 25, 1638",
    "place": "Massa",
    "archival": "ARSI, FG 741, 261",
    "excerpt": "I ceased for some time to press Your Paternity for the mission of the Indies, from which for my sins I have deserved for this time to be excluded. But I do not believe that for this Your Paternity denies me leave to continue weeping before you, and to signify to you the desires that God gives me. I have already taken up Rhetoric, as Your Paternity and the Father Provincial have commanded me, and I would have done so with every other exercise, however lowly, had it been the will of obedience; nevertheless I pray you to be pleased that in this time too you give ear to His Divine Majesty, who calls me and prepares me for the other Mission, which they say should be near at hand with the coming of the Father Procurator of China, who is on the voyage.",
    "kind": "abridged",
    "full_paras": [
     "I ceased for some time to press Your Paternity for the mission of the Indies, from which for my sins I have deserved for this time to be excluded. But I do not believe that for this Your Paternity denies me leave to continue weeping before you, and to signify to you the desires that God gives me. I have already taken up Rhetoric, as Your Paternity and the Father Provincial have commanded me, and I would have done so with every other exercise, however lowly, had it been the will of obedience; nevertheless I pray you to be pleased that in this time too you give ear to His Divine Majesty, who calls me and prepares me for the other Mission, which they say should be near at hand with the coming of the Father Procurator of China, who is on the voyage. This letter of mine will reach Your Paternity on the feast of Saint Francis; I pray you to give him to me as companion, to follow him on so glorious a voyage. For the rest, I am praying Our Lord to grant every grace to Your Paternity, and I beseech you to give me your blessing, and the most desired placet to my most ardent desires."
    ]
   },
   {
    "n": 101,
    "year": 1639,
    "date": "July 20, 1639",
    "place": "Massa",
    "archival": "ARSI, FG 742, 99",
    "excerpt": "The most glorious death of Father Marcello Mastrilli has moved the whole world; and I believe that Your Paternity has received, on this occasion, thousands of letters from persons asking for the Indies. Mine come late, and after all the others, because they are ashamed to appear before you: first, for my sins, and second, for the many rejections. Nevertheless, though unworthy and rebuffed, I return to declare my desires once more — desires which will never end but with death. Now that the coming of the Father Procurator of China is awaited, asking it with these infinite [desires], I beg Your Paternity, for [the love] of so great glory of God and of the Society in those parts, not to rebuff me again.",
    "kind": "abridged",
    "full_paras": [
     "The most glorious death of Father Marcello Mastrilli has moved the whole world; and I believe that Your Paternity has received, on this occasion, thousands of letters from persons asking for the Indies. Mine come late, and after all the others, because they are ashamed to appear before you: first, for my sins, and second, for the many rejections. Nevertheless, though unworthy and rebuffed, I return to declare my desires once more — desires which will never end but with death. Now that the coming of the Father Procurator of China is awaited, asking it with these infinite [desires], I beg Your Paternity, for [the love] of so great glory of God and of the Society in those parts, not to rebuff me again. Let it suffice, as punishment for my shortcomings, that I once saw myself near to so great a grandeur and was cast down from it. Oh, if I could show Your Paternity the tears that for this cause I shed before God, how they would soften you! But I hope that Saint Francis Xavier will present them to you on my behalf, and will suggest to you those reasons that I do not know how to write to you. Wherewith, begging Your Paternity to give me your blessing, I end."
    ]
   },
   {
    "n": 103,
    "year": 1639,
    "date": "October 27, 1639",
    "place": "Massa",
    "archival": "ARSI, FG 742, 171",
    "excerpt": "Although for the present there seems no near opportunity for the Indies, nevertheless, to show my desire and the great affection that God deigns to communicate to me for this most glorious Mission, I return to make new instance of it to Your Paternity. I hope that so many and such importunate requests will not be burdensome to you, any more than prayers are to God; and I have faith that my letters, if they could have no efficacy on any other account, will at least have it by their multitude. Pardon, Your Paternity, for the love of God, my excessive importunity — which, though it be very great, is yet little in comparison with the infinite hunger I have to run into those provinces where God so long ago began to call me, and continues every day to call me.",
    "kind": "abridged",
    "full_paras": [
     "Although for the present there seems no near opportunity for the Indies, nevertheless, to show my desire and the great affection that God deigns to communicate to me for this most glorious Mission, I return to make new instance of it to Your Paternity. I hope that so many and such importunate requests will not be burdensome to you, any more than prayers are to God; and I have faith that my letters, if they could have no efficacy on any other account, will at least have it by their multitude. Pardon, Your Paternity, for the love of God, my excessive importunity — which, though it be very great, is yet little in comparison with the infinite hunger I have to run into those provinces where God so long ago began to call me, and continues every day to call me. For the rest, let Your Paternity consider that only the hope of being consoled with the first Mission that shall go keeps me somewhat at peace in this life. Nor will the benignity of Your Paternity let me be left behind this next time, as my sins would deserve. Wherewith, begging Your Paternity to give me your blessing, I end."
    ]
   },
   {
    "n": 104,
    "year": 1639,
    "date": "November 1, 1639",
    "place": "Massa",
    "archival": "ARSI, FG 742, 175",
    "excerpt": "I wish to renew with this letter all my ancient requests, and all the tears with which I have wearied for so long the most benign ears of Your Paternity. For the love of God, and by as much as you desire His glory, designate me for India; and do not answer me — my great desire makes me bold to set laws upon your charity — do not answer me that there is at present no opportunity, for Your Paternity can designate me now and advise me of when there shall be opportunity, which I hope will be soon. O God, why do I not find myself in Rome, cast at your most benign feet, showing my affection and the desire that has so long burned in my heart?",
    "kind": "abridged",
    "full_paras": [
     "I wish to renew with this letter all my ancient requests, and all the tears with which I have wearied for so long the most benign ears of Your Paternity. For the love of God, and by as much as you desire His glory, designate me for India; and do not answer me — my great desire makes me bold to set laws upon your charity — do not answer me that there is at present no opportunity, for Your Paternity can designate me now and advise me of when there shall be opportunity, which I hope will be soon. O God, why do I not find myself in Rome, cast at your most benign feet, showing my affection and the desire that has so long burned in my heart? Let Your Paternity at least consider it, and at last console me; and if you would show me a sign of your benignity, send me the crucifix that you were to send me when I was once before admonished — or at least declare that the crucifix I keep shall stand in place of the one owed to me for India. O how much I ask, and how little I deserve! Yet I do not repent of having asked. Meanwhile I beg my great Apostle and Captain Francis Xavier to plead my cause, since so many of my letters can avail so little by reason of my sins. Wherewith, humbly asking your blessing, I end."
    ]
   },
   {
    "n": 132,
    "year": 1641,
    "date": "July 13, 1641",
    "place": "Naples",
    "archival": "ARSI, FG 743, 97",
    "excerpt": "I return to Your Paternity to make once more my petition for the Indies, and to recall to you my ardent desires, continued for so long a time. Already the Father Procurator of China finds himself in Rome, and I find myself disengaged from every other occupation by the particular providence of God, who wishes in this manner to show that He has kept me for the East; nothing remains to me but the blessing of Your Paternity, with which to set myself on the journey — of which he wished to give me a pledge a year and a half ago by sending me the Crucifix of the Indies.",
    "kind": "abridged",
    "full_paras": [
     "I return to Your Paternity to make once more my petition for the Indies, and to recall to you my ardent desires, continued for so long a time. Already the Father Procurator of China finds himself in Rome, and I find myself disengaged from every other occupation by the particular providence of God, who wishes in this manner to show that He has kept me for the East; nothing remains to me but the blessing of Your Paternity, with which to set myself on the journey — of which he wished to give me a pledge a year and a half ago by sending me the Crucifix of the Indies.",
     "I pray His Divine Majesty that He may show to you the tears I shed, and the continual sighs for this end, and the prayers that each day I make to God, that they may move your heart to grant me the grace; and I hope that after so long a time you will grant it to me — at least propter importunitatem (on account of importunity). For this importunity I ask your pardon; yet I believe it is welcome to you, since it concerns a matter of such great service and glory of God. With this, praying you to give me your blessing, I end."
    ]
   },
   {
    "n": 139,
    "year": 1641,
    "date": "September 7, 1641",
    "place": "Naples",
    "archival": "ARSI, FG 743, 161",
    "excerpt": "I have shown myself perhaps a little cold in writing, and in making new petitions to Your Paternity to obtain the grace of the Indies, because it seemed there was no certainty of the Mission; but now that so many have been chosen by Your Paternity, I weep for my negligence, in not having manifested my desire infinitely many times. I trust, however, in the extreme charity of Your Paternity that, with all this, you will not leave me behind, nor forget the petitions I have made at other times, nor the promises that, with the greatest benignity, Your Paternity has deigned to make me.",
    "kind": "abridged",
    "full_paras": [
     "I have shown myself perhaps a little cold in writing, and in making new petitions to Your Paternity to obtain the grace of the Indies, because it seemed there was no certainty of the Mission; but now that so many have been chosen by Your Paternity, I weep for my negligence, in not having manifested my desire infinitely many times. I trust, however, in the extreme charity of Your Paternity that, with all this, you will not leave me behind, nor forget the petitions I have made at other times, nor the promises that, with the greatest benignity, Your Paternity has deigned to make me.",
     "My Father, the longing that God gives me to run to the East, and to expose myself to every torment for the glory of His Divine Majesty, is such that I {dare not} explain it in words; and then it has lasted for so long a time, with the same fervor, that it seems impossible it is not a thing of God. Your Paternity has had, for so many years, continual letters from me; I supplicate you to console me, and to send me there, where by so many signs, with so many calls, God wills that I go — and I would show you the tears that for this end I continually shed. I know not how to say more, except that this, on earth, is my only paradise; and so, with all the efficacy that I can, with my face to the ground, I pray you to grant me the grace; and I shall see myself bound, for all the time of my life, to pray always to God for Your Paternity — from whom I shall have received the greatest benefit that can be obtained on earth. With this, praying you to give me your blessing, I end."
    ]
   },
   {
    "n": 142,
    "year": 1641,
    "date": "September 14, 1641",
    "place": "Naples",
    "archival": "ARSI, FG 743, 171",
    "excerpt": "Before the reply can come, I add a second letter to Your Paternity, and on this occasion I beg you to grant me license to multiply them as many as I can, and perhaps to be importunate. Let Your Paternity alone read them; for the rest, I care for no other reply than the last, when I am notified. I return to making my usual petitions for the Indies, and I renew the tears that I have shed in so many other letters; nor shall I ever tire of writing while I have a hand, nor of writing and weeping while I have eyes, to obtain a thing which, once obtained, can bring me beatitude.",
    "kind": "abridged",
    "full_paras": [
     "Before the reply can come, I add a second letter to Your Paternity, and on this occasion I beg you to grant me license to multiply them as many as I can, and perhaps to be importunate. Let Your Paternity alone read them; for the rest, I care for no other reply than the last, when I am notified. I return to making my usual petitions for the Indies, and I renew the tears that I have shed in so many other letters; nor shall I ever tire of writing while I have a hand, nor of writing and weeping while I have eyes, to obtain a thing which, once obtained, can bring me beatitude. I do not know how to explain my desire to you further, except by telling you that in this life I have nothing that I desire except this, and God seems to give me to understand that this is the only means of my salvation, and of the salvation of many. I see well that this is such a grace that others than my merits would be needed to obtain it. But if that is what is sought, who is there that merits it? And then, if it is not otherwise owed to me, at least the continual petitions of so long a time must work something. I pray, and I conjure Your Paternity by all the saints of Paradise, that at last she bend to so many petitions, and to so many tears, and grant me in this her good pleasure — which I now offer to pay a thousand times over, by weight of blood. I finish by praying Your Paternity to grant me her benediction, and the license to run where God calls me."
    ]
   },
   {
    "n": 145,
    "year": 1641,
    "date": "October 15, 1641",
    "place": "Naples",
    "archival": "ARSI, FG 743, 230",
    "excerpt": "I would not dare with others to do what I do with Your Paternity, but I know well to whom I write; and so, although the sentence seems to have been given, I appeal nonetheless to those bowels of a father which cannot but be moved, given their lovingkindness and benignity, at so many voices, so many groans, so many tears of a son of his so afflicted. That I should not go to the Indies? This is a sentence for me so painful that it cannot come (forgive me, Your Paternity, this word) from that father who loves me so, as I have known — having endured me in the Company for so long a time with so many failings.",
    "kind": "abridged",
    "full_paras": [
     "I would not dare with others to do what I do with Your Paternity, but I know well to whom I write; and so, although the sentence seems to have been given, I appeal nonetheless to those bowels of a father which cannot but be moved, given their lovingkindness and benignity, at so many voices, so many groans, so many tears of a son of his so afflicted. That I should not go to the Indies? This is a sentence for me so painful that it cannot come (forgive me, Your Paternity, this word) from that father who loves me so, as I have known — having endured me in the Company for so long a time with so many failings. My Father, and why does Your Paternity not send me? The Father Procurator wants me and says there is a place for me; my Father Provincial does not hinder me. Only the will of Your Paternity has to be moved. She being so loving, and so benign, that there is no one who does not praise her: she gave me hope that when all the world were contrary to me, she alone should favor me; and now, whence comes it that this one alone opposes so many tears of mine? The last two letters of Your Paternity console me in some part. One tells me to continue knocking; another that she does not exclude me. Little remains after these premises to write me that Yes, which can send me to paradise. And I, kneeling before a crucifix, all tears, all sighs, and all sorrow, pray her, for the same Lord, that with her benediction she write it to me, while to the holy Sacrifices and prayers of Your Paternity I commend myself."
    ]
   },
   {
    "n": 162,
    "year": 1643,
    "date": "June 13, 1643",
    "place": "Naples",
    "archival": "ARSI, FG 744, 91",
    "excerpt": "After so many petitions made through all the years I have been in the Company, I now begin to renew them on the occasion of the coming of the Father Procurator of the Indies, Father Francesco Carvaglio. I know that Your Paternity, for the affection you bear me and for your charity, will not have me suffer any more. Truly, the other times I have found myself occupied in one thing or another; now Our Lord has made me quite free, so that in this very moment I could leap to the ship and fly off to where Our Lord calls me.",
    "kind": "abridged",
    "full_paras": [
     "After so many petitions made through all the years I have been in the Company, I now begin to renew them on the occasion of the coming of the Father Procurator of the Indies, Father Francesco Carvaglio. I know that Your Paternity, for the affection you bear me and for your charity, will not have me suffer any more. Truly, the other times I have found myself occupied in one thing or another; now Our Lord has made me quite free, so that in this very moment I could leap to the ship and fly off to where Our Lord calls me. My Father, with infinite tears I throw myself at the feet of Your Paternity and beg you to console me. Send me where for so many years I have sighed, where so many companions call me, where God seems in every way to will me, and where Your Paternity has deigned to destine me, sending me the crucifix as pledge, and with almost infinite promises. May Our Lord grant Your Paternity long life for His glory, and to me only as much as will let me reach the Indies and shed my blood for the faith. I close by begging you to give me your blessing."
    ]
   },
   {
    "n": 163,
    "year": 1643,
    "date": "July 25, 1643",
    "place": "Naples",
    "archival": "ARSI, FG 744, 114",
    "excerpt": "I return to make known to Your Paternity my supreme and only desire for the Indies, for I know that shortly Your Paternity will make the most fortunate choice of subjects for that most glorious Mission. And though each day I see myself more unworthy of it for my sins, yet I say to Your Paternity what I say to God: Si iniquitates observaveris Domine, domine quis sustinebit? (if thou, Lord, shouldst mark iniquities, Lord, who shall stand?) This is the seventh year in which I never let an occasion pass to do all I can and to take every means to attain what I desire; and what more has Your Paternity to do to see whether this is the will of God who calls me?",
    "kind": "abridged",
    "full_paras": [
     "I return to make known to Your Paternity my supreme and only desire for the Indies, for I know that shortly Your Paternity will make the most fortunate choice of subjects for that most glorious Mission. And though each day I see myself more unworthy of it for my sins, yet I say to Your Paternity what I say to God: Si iniquitates observaveris Domine, domine quis sustinebit? (if thou, Lord, shouldst mark iniquities, Lord, who shall stand?) This is the seventh year in which I never let an occasion pass to do all I can and to take every means to attain what I desire; and what more has Your Paternity to do to see whether this is the will of God who calls me? I renew now, in this last occasion, all my past petitions, and I lay before her all my tears, and I beg you, by that Lord who must judge me, to hear the voices of one who for so many years has cried out continually. I close with Your Paternity, but my tears do not end, which I cast continuously at the feet of the crucifix for this which I call beatitude in this life. Wherewith I beg you to give me your blessing."
    ]
   },
   {
    "n": 165,
    "year": 1643,
    "date": "October 3, 1643",
    "place": "Naples",
    "archival": "ARSI, FG 744, 142",
    "excerpt": "I render more thanks to Your Paternity than I have [...] — you have at last deigned to admit so many of my tears, so many sighs, and so many prayers, telling me that this grace was owed me for the infinite insistence I have made. And I answer that even if truly infinite, it would be little, for the grace I have received has no price that can repay it. It was only the benignity of Your Paternity, and my most holy Father and Apostle of the Indies, St. Francis Xavier, who held in his hands the keys of the treasures of Heaven and opened them and made this great prodigality toward one who, after so many years, ought to have been in hell.",
    "kind": "abridged",
    "full_paras": [
     "I render more thanks to Your Paternity than I have [...] — you have at last deigned to admit so many of my tears, so many sighs, and so many prayers, telling me that this grace was owed me for the infinite insistence I have made. And I answer that even if truly infinite, it would be little, for the grace I have received has no price that can repay it. It was only the benignity of Your Paternity, and my most holy Father and Apostle of the Indies, St. Francis Xavier, who held in his hands the keys of the treasures of Heaven and opened them and made this great prodigality toward one who, after so many years, ought to have been in hell. I meanwhile pray His Divine Majesty to pay Your Paternity the recompense of so signal a grace, for which not only have I no power to make satisfaction, but I see myself unable even to pay it in words. In prayer, however, I shall never forget so obliging a person who has bound me with so much. And if His Divine Majesty shall deign of His mercy to accept this blood: Your Paternity shall have great part in it. In closing I beg you to give me your blessing and to obtain for me from my Protector St. Francis, with the Indies he has given me, the spirit of an Indian."
    ]
   }
  ]
 },
 {
  "id": "hist-64",
  "fictional": false,
  "name": "Daniello (1608-1685) Bartoli",
  "author_raw": "Bartoli, Daniello (1608-1685)",
  "age": null,
  "yearsInSociety": null,
  "formation": null,
  "studies": null,
  "skills": [],
  "health": null,
  "assignment": null,
  "destination": "east-indies",
  "dossier": "Wrote 3 letters from Parma (1627–1635) petitioning the General for the foreign missions; requested destinations: East Indies (2), China (1).",
  "letters": [
   {
    "n": 64,
    "year": 1627,
    "date": "February 2, 1627",
    "place": "Parma",
    "archival": "ARSI, FG 738, 7",
    "excerpt": "The vehement desires which, by His infinite goodness, the Lord God deigns to communicate to me — to amplify as far as possible throughout the whole World His glory, spending, after every sort of fatigue and hardship, even my life amid the greatest torments — compel me to have recourse to Your Paternity, in whose hands, after the Lord God, lies the granting me their fulfillment. I confess coram Domino (before the Lord) to Your Paternity that, by the grace which His Divine Majesty at times grants me of knowing some particle of what His Divine being merits, all the glory which His Saints have hitherto rendered Him seems to me nothing in comparison with that which it gives me the desire to procure for Him.",
    "kind": "abridged",
    "full_paras": [
     "The vehement desires which, by His infinite goodness, the Lord God deigns to communicate to me — to amplify as far as possible throughout the whole World His glory, spending, after every sort of fatigue and hardship, even my life amid the greatest torments — compel me to have recourse to Your Paternity, in whose hands, after the Lord God, lies the granting me their fulfillment. I confess coram Domino (before the Lord) to Your Paternity that, by the grace which His Divine Majesty at times grants me of knowing some particle of what His Divine being merits, all the glory which His Saints have hitherto rendered Him seems to me nothing in comparison with that which it gives me the desire to procure for Him. And for this, though indifferent, yet with the greatest affection I offer myself at his feet as the most unworthy instrument of the Divine Glory in the conversion of the Infidels, in whatever part of the World Your Paternity shall judge to send me — desirous, whatever I may be, of enduring to this end every sort of suffering, and, if it please the Lord God, death.",
     "Nor does the clear knowledge and continual experience of my very little spirit and total unfitness for so great an enterprise at all draw me back from asking so great a thing: rather, just as for three years it persuaded me not to dare to disclose it to Your Paternity, so now it emboldens me all the more, knowing the custom of the Lord God, who at times is wont to choose for the greatest enterprises those least worthy of them. Cast therefore at your feet, my most loving Father, with all possible affection I offer myself to you anew — wholly indifferent in all things, and in all things eager to apply myself as soon as possible to the labors of the Indies, and to the desired end of the Divine Glory in Martyrdom. And in conclusion, praying Him for you from heaven every fullness of the Holy Spirit, I humbly ask, prostrate, your paternal blessing."
    ]
   },
   {
    "n": 66,
    "year": 1628,
    "date": "February 1, 1628",
    "place": "Parma",
    "archival": "ARSI, FG 738, 189",
    "excerpt": "Behold me cast once more at your feet by the most ardent desire which, by His mercy, the Lord God gives me to amplify His Holy Name in aiding the infidels, and amid the greatest hardships and sufferings of persecutions. O if, my most loving Father, I might find a place in the first occasions of departing toward harvests and treasures so precious! I feel great confidence in saying it, asking it of you as the first grace, to the glory of these our newly blessed Martyr Brothers: speak, Your Paternity, and in their name grant it to me.",
    "kind": "abridged",
    "full_paras": [
     "Behold me cast once more at your feet by the most ardent desire which, by His mercy, the Lord God gives me to amplify His Holy Name in aiding the infidels, and amid the greatest hardships and sufferings of persecutions. O if, my most loving Father, I might find a place in the first occasions of departing toward harvests and treasures so precious! I feel great confidence in saying it, asking it of you as the first grace, to the glory of these our newly blessed Martyr Brothers: speak, Your Paternity, and in their name grant it to me. My most loving Father, the infinity of glory which that incomprehensible being of the Lord God merits — the sole motive of this desire of mine, had from the time I knew Him — would require of me infinite years and infinite work: ah, for His love do not deny me at least, Your Paternity, these my first [years], which I will spend, oh how willingly and wholeheartedly, in whatever service of those who occupy themselves in so precious and Divine an office as the conversion of the Gentiles.",
     "There is England, Japan, China, and so many other barbarous and heretical lands: may I too, least of all who ask you such a grace, find in so great a vastness some place where, according to my desire, I may spend my life in the conversion of souls and finish it in the fire. To obtain this, cast prostrate at his feet, I ask your holy blessing, awaiting as soon as possible the fruit of it."
    ]
   },
   {
    "n": 85,
    "year": 1635,
    "date": "October 5, 1635",
    "place": "Parma",
    "archival": "ARSI, FG 740, 363",
    "excerpt": "I have consoled all this year my desire with the hope of the arrival there of the Father Procurator of Cochin, to whom Your Paternity promised Father Antonio Magaglianes to give me as companion for the mission of the Indies. Now that he has arrived, here I am, after so many other times, prostrate anew at your feet, where with all the affection of my soul, for the love of the holy Father Xavier, I supplicate you to console at last my desire to consume myself wholly in the aid of those barbarians, and to shed even, should the Lord God do me the grace, my blood for His love and in witness of His holy faith.",
    "kind": "abridged",
    "full_paras": [
     "I have consoled all this year my desire with the hope of the arrival there of the Father Procurator of Cochin, to whom Your Paternity promised Father Antonio Magaglianes to give me as companion for the mission of the Indies. Now that he has arrived, here I am, after so many other times, prostrate anew at your feet, where with all the affection of my soul, for the love of the holy Father Xavier, I supplicate you to console at last my desire to consume myself wholly in the aid of those barbarians, and to shed even, should the Lord God do me the grace, my blood for His love and in witness of His holy faith. This desire has remained ever most lively in my heart, and has grown since I entered this holy order, twelve years now. This I offer to Your Paternity, and for its fulfillment I supplicate you with all my heart. Do not delay, for the love of God, to a later occasion to do me the grace, but from this hour give what remains of my life wholly to the holy Apostle Xavier and consecrate it to the good of the Indies; where, if you grant me to go there, I shall never cease to show myself grateful to Your Paternity, offering in recognition of so great a grace the best part of the labors that apostolic life brings with it. Wherewith, in conclusion, prostrate anew at your feet, I ask most humbly, with the desired grace, your paternal blessing."
    ]
   }
  ]
 },
 {
  "id": "hist-65",
  "fictional": false,
  "name": "Francesco Giuseppe (1612-1672) Bressani",
  "author_raw": "Bressani, Francesco Giuseppe (1612-1672)",
  "age": null,
  "yearsInSociety": null,
  "formation": null,
  "studies": null,
  "skills": [],
  "health": null,
  "assignment": null,
  "destination": "east-indies",
  "dossier": "Wrote 5 letters from place not stated (1627–1636) petitioning the General for the foreign missions; requested destination: East Indies (5).",
  "letters": [
   {
    "n": 65,
    "year": 1627,
    "date": "July 1627",
    "place": null,
    "archival": "ARSI, FG 738, 126",
    "excerpt": "Having heard that whoever desires to go to the Indies should now, there being a convenient occasion and need of men, make it known by giving a memorial — though in me I am certain (as my Superior can well know) that I have neither the virtue nor the qualities requisite for going there — nevertheless, both because I trust that, if I were sent, the strength of holy obedience would supply the lack, and also because I am convinced that whoever has some desire should manifest it: I manifest to Your Most Reverend Paternity that I would most willingly go both to the Indies and to any other mission whatsoever, from a desire both to detach myself from the sight of kinsfolk and country — other attachments toward which things, it seems to me, are not yet dead in me — and of Saint Andrew's, to which I am in some way attached, and from a desire to aid souls, according to my ability, in conformity with my vocation.",
    "kind": "abridged",
    "full_paras": [
     "Having heard that whoever desires to go to the Indies should now, there being a convenient occasion and need of men, make it known by giving a memorial — though in me I am certain (as my Superior can well know) that I have neither the virtue nor the qualities requisite for going there — nevertheless, both because I trust that, if I were sent, the strength of holy obedience would supply the lack, and also because I am convinced that whoever has some desire should manifest it: I manifest to Your Most Reverend Paternity that I would most willingly go both to the Indies and to any other mission whatsoever, from a desire both to detach myself from the sight of kinsfolk and country — other attachments toward which things, it seems to me, are not yet dead in me — and of Saint Andrew's, to which I am in some way attached, and from a desire to aid souls, according to my ability, in conformity with my vocation.",
     "Wherefore I pray Your Most Reverend Paternity that, if you judge that the greater honor and glory of God — with all my imperfections and insufficiencies presupposed — lies in detaching me from here, where I willingly remain, you deign to grant it to me, offering myself therefore to do as and how Your Most Reverend Paternity shall order."
    ]
   },
   {
    "n": 68,
    "year": 1628,
    "date": "September 1628",
    "place": null,
    "archival": "ARSI, FG 738, 286",
    "excerpt": "I would not have had the boldness — considering my natural, spiritual, and moral unfitnesses, of weak health and little or no virtue — to propose to Your Most Reverend Paternity the desire which Our Lord God, on the occasion of reading the Life of the glorious Saint Francis Xavier, deigned to communicate to me, of the Mission to the Indies, if on the other hand, the interior Reasons which made this fulfillment difficult for me having been overcome by divine mercy, I had not known the obligation of corresponding to the divine Inspirations, and had not seen how God at times takes pleasure, to show His Omnipotence and goodness more, and for His other secret Judgments, in making use, for the aid of souls, of the weak and feeble, and those at times esteemed of no worth.",
    "kind": "abridged",
    "full_paras": [
     "I would not have had the boldness — considering my natural, spiritual, and moral unfitnesses, of weak health and little or no virtue — to propose to Your Most Reverend Paternity the desire which Our Lord God, on the occasion of reading the Life of the glorious Saint Francis Xavier, deigned to communicate to me, of the Mission to the Indies, if on the other hand, the interior Reasons which made this fulfillment difficult for me having been overcome by divine mercy, I had not known the obligation of corresponding to the divine Inspirations, and had not seen how God at times takes pleasure, to show His Omnipotence and goodness more, and for His other secret Judgments, in making use, for the aid of souls, of the weak and feeble, and those at times esteemed of no worth.",
     "Wherefore, representing to Your Most Reverend Paternity this desire of mine just as it is, with the present occasions, I pray that, if you judge the execution of it the greater service of His Divine Majesty, you do not fail to grant it to me, trusting in His Divine Majesty, who facit ea quae non sunt tamquam ea quae sunt (who makes the things that are not as though they were), to overcome all the difficulties that might oppose me in this. And with this, asking your holy blessing, I pray His Divine Majesty to deign to inspire in you what is to His greater Glory."
    ]
   },
   {
    "n": 70,
    "year": 1628,
    "date": "October 1628",
    "place": null,
    "archival": "ARSI, FG 738, 305",
    "excerpt": "Having already three times proposed to Your Paternity the desire which His Divine Majesty has deigned to give me for the Indies — and having, by my negligence and carelessness, not sought a response — now, so as not to have to render account to God of having trampled His Inspirations by not cooperating with them, I have resolved, as I do, to pray Your Paternity to deign, if you judge the fulfillment of this desire to be in any way, for the Reasons stated at other times, a service of His Divine Majesty, to deign to grant it to me.",
    "kind": "abridged",
    "full_paras": [
     "Having already three times proposed to Your Paternity the desire which His Divine Majesty has deigned to give me for the Indies — and having, by my negligence and carelessness, not sought a response — now, so as not to have to render account to God of having trampled His Inspirations by not cooperating with them, I have resolved, as I do, to pray Your Paternity to deign, if you judge the fulfillment of this desire to be in any way, for the Reasons stated at other times, a service of His Divine Majesty, to deign to grant it to me.",
     "And if even now, by reason of age and studies etc., it should not seem fitting to you, at least at the end of the present Year of Physics — which I believe would be a most convenient time — I pray you to grant it to me, for that love which you bear to your sons and the desire you have for their perfection, and for all their greater good and glory of God.",
     "Trusting in the divine Omnipotence and goodness, and in the intercession of the Most Blessed Virgin, who will give me the spiritual and bodily virtue and strength necessary for this — with which, praying His Divine Majesty for abundant graces to bear the burden he bears, I offer myself to you most indifferent to all things, to be placed and employed in whatever shall be the greater service of His Divine Majesty and the salvation of mine and of souls."
    ]
   },
   {
    "n": 87,
    "year": 1636,
    "date": "May 1636",
    "place": null,
    "archival": "ARSI, FG 740, 327",
    "excerpt": "As I never feel my lukewarmness more kindled than when that holy desire is revived in me which, against all my merit, the Holy Spirit kindled in my heart — as I have always believed — for the holy mission of the Indies, and as I never conceive any good desire without this one at once renewing itself with it; so I think myself obliged often to turn my thoughts to it and to propose it warmly, until Your Paternity, to whom after God I must turn, grants me its effect, lest my many demerits hinder the execution of the divine will and the possibility of in fact more abundantly sharing the divine liberality which, with its holy inspirations, has already offered me favors so great.",
    "kind": "abridged",
    "full_paras": [
     "As I never feel my lukewarmness more kindled than when that holy desire is revived in me which, against all my merit, the Holy Spirit kindled in my heart — as I have always believed — for the holy mission of the Indies, and as I never conceive any good desire without this one at once renewing itself with it; so I think myself obliged often to turn my thoughts to it and to propose it warmly, until Your Paternity, to whom after God I must turn, grants me its effect, lest my many demerits hinder the execution of the divine will and the possibility of in fact more abundantly sharing the divine liberality which, with its holy inspirations, has already offered me favors so great. All the more would I wish to be able to express to Your Paternity the liveliness of this desire, the more I am convinced by experience that whatever little good I think of doing, I at once prove a particular recompense of graces from that Lord, so liberal that He promised not to leave even a cup of water without reward. This is all my confidence in this matter: that if, with His blessing and that of Your Paternity, I should undertake this holy voyage, I would receive some particular measure of graces, with whose aid I might spend all the little that the Lord will give me of life with that spirit which I have not had until now and which is necessary to a true son of the Company. Set me, Your Paternity, in this occasion of so great a good, for that affection and desire which you have for the salvation of your sons, even the most unworthy and miserable.",
     "And while I add nothing more, that I may not weary you further, believe that if I know how to express little desire in this letter, the desire I keep in my heart is not little, for the reasons at other times proposed to you; which I pray that the working of the Holy Spirit, who in these days descends from heaven, may make efficacious. And while I pray that you may have great abundance of Him, I hope to have some share in it myself through the prayers of Your Paternity. Quam Deus [...] (which God [...])"
    ]
   },
   {
    "n": 88,
    "year": 1636,
    "date": "July 1636",
    "place": null,
    "archival": "ARSI, FG 740, 444",
    "excerpt": "There is not yet extinguished amid the ice of my so many failings that little warmth with which the Lord kindled my heart when He gave me the holy desire of the Indies. It is perhaps that little [of ... covered ...] but there will come a time [...] when [it shall appear luminous]. Meanwhile may Your Paternity [...] me, if not with a final concession of the grace, at least with a firm hope of it; and I shall be eternally mindful of such a benefit, which I wholly believe pertains to the salvation of my soul, and I shall be under a more particular obligation to pray the Lord for Your Paternity.",
    "kind": "abridged",
    "full_paras": [
     "There is not yet extinguished amid the ice of my so many failings that little warmth with which the Lord kindled my heart when He gave me the holy desire of the Indies. It is perhaps that little [of ... covered ...] but there will come a time [...] when [it shall appear luminous]. Meanwhile may Your Paternity [...] me, if not with a final concession of the grace, at least with a firm hope of it; and I shall be eternally mindful of such a benefit, which I wholly believe pertains to the salvation of my soul, and I shall be under a more particular obligation to pray the Lord for Your Paternity. Quam Deus [...] (which God [...])"
    ]
