  "dossier": "Wrote 2 letters from Cremona, Genoa (1628–1640) petitioning the General for the foreign missions; requested destination: East Indies (2).",
  "letters": [
   {
    "n": 67,
    "year": 1628,
    "date": "July 24, 1628",
    "place": "Genoa",
    "archival": "ARSI, FG 738, 246",
    "excerpt": "My having up to now not manifested to Your Paternity my Vocation for the Indies was not because God has newly sent it to me — for since I asked to enter the Society I always asked with this intention — but by counsel of my Father Rector of the Novitiate, who told me I ought first to apply myself to grounding myself well in virtue. Now at last, though unworthy, having been admitted to the first degree of the Society, and finding myself at the beginning of my studies — a Milanese by country, aged nineteen, a Humanist of two years in Letters — I have resolved, with the counsel of my Superiors, to disclose to Your Paternity that I truly desire to spend my life in whatever part of the Indies, and above all in that one where, for the completion of my labors, I might for reward shed my blood for Him who so lavishly shed His for me on the Cross.",
    "kind": "abridged",
    "full_paras": [
     "My having up to now not manifested to Your Paternity my Vocation for the Indies was not because God has newly sent it to me — for since I asked to enter the Society I always asked with this intention — but by counsel of my Father Rector of the Novitiate, who told me I ought first to apply myself to grounding myself well in virtue. Now at last, though unworthy, having been admitted to the first degree of the Society, and finding myself at the beginning of my studies — a Milanese by country, aged nineteen, a Humanist of two years in Letters — I have resolved, with the counsel of my Superiors, to disclose to Your Paternity that I truly desire to spend my life in whatever part of the Indies, and above all in that one where, for the completion of my labors, I might for reward shed my blood for Him who so lavishly shed His for me on the Cross.",
     "And this I have always desired, and have always asked of the Lord with great insistence in my prayers, and I feel I have from God strength, both spiritual and bodily, to endure whatever fatigue and hardship. Hoping then to obtain so great a grace, I come with this my [letter] humbly to offer myself to Your Paternity with all largeness of heart and readiness of mind, suppliantly asking that you deign to fulfill this desire of mine as soon as possible, to the greater glory of God and my own good; with which I make an end, making you humble reverence and commending myself to your Holy Sacrifices."
    ]
   },
   {
    "n": 113,
    "year": 1640,
    "date": "May 5, 1640",
    "place": "Cremona",
    "archival": "ARSI, FG 742, 272",
    "excerpt": "Now that I find myself, by the grace of the Lord, at the end both of my studies and of my fifth year of school, I come anew to beseech Your Paternity for the grace for which I have beseeched him at other times, both by letters and in person — that is, the Mission to the Indies. Let Your Paternity believe me, for I confess it to him before the Lord: the voice of God, which calls me thither, has never ceased in me; and I am by now certain that nothing moves me but the pure desire of serving Him in those parts, and of leaving for His love whatever contentment I might have in these.",
    "kind": "abridged",
    "full_paras": [
     "Now that I find myself, by the grace of the Lord, at the end both of my studies and of my fifth year of school, I come anew to beseech Your Paternity for the grace for which I have beseeched him at other times, both by letters and in person — that is, the Mission to the Indies. Let Your Paternity believe me, for I confess it to him before the Lord: the voice of God, which calls me thither, has never ceased in me; and I am by now certain that nothing moves me but the pure desire of serving Him in those parts, and of leaving for His love whatever contentment I might have in these. Such has always been my thought from the beginning of my entrance into Religion. I therefore pray Your Paternity with all humility that he would, with his blessing, grant my petition, to which I shall always hope in the Lord to be able to correspond, since He makes me so determinedly propose it to myself. I am no longer fixed upon one place rather than another; I place myself entirely in his paternal hands, and I shall hold myself fully satisfied with whichever place I am assigned. Hoping for which, I humbly reverence him and commend myself with all my heart to his holy Sacrifices."
    ]
   }
  ]
 },
 {
  "id": "hist-71",
  "fictional": false,
