  "dossier": "Wrote 5 letters from Milan (1629–1633) petitioning the General for the foreign missions; requested destinations: Japan (3), East Indies (2).",
  "letters": [
   {
    "n": 71,
    "year": 1629,
    "date": "December 25, 1629",
    "place": "Milan",
    "archival": "ARSI, FG 738, 402",
    "excerpt": "So great is the longing and desire that Our Lord has communicated to me to go and assist the souls who remain in the Indies, and to shed my blood for love of my Lord, who first shed His for me, that after having waited two years, He has now so increased it in me that, did I not offer myself at the feet of Your Paternity, most ready for its execution, I would judge myself to be manifestly contradicting the divine will, which calls me to great things, as appears from the signal favors by which He has delivered me from many and various infirmities and manifest dangers of life, endured even from the cradle, and [by endowing me with] a constitution and nature well suited to the labors and hardships of those countries — not without manifest indication that He has reserved me to spend my life and shed my blood to His glory and in aid of souls.",
    "kind": "abridged",
    "full_paras": [
     "So great is the longing and desire that Our Lord has communicated to me to go and assist the souls who remain in the Indies, and to shed my blood for love of my Lord, who first shed His for me, that after having waited two years, He has now so increased it in me that, did I not offer myself at the feet of Your Paternity, most ready for its execution, I would judge myself to be manifestly contradicting the divine will, which calls me to great things, as appears from the signal favors by which He has delivered me from many and various infirmities and manifest dangers of life, endured even from the cradle, and [by endowing me with] a constitution and nature well suited to the labors and hardships of those countries — not without manifest indication that He has reserved me to spend my life and shed my blood to His glory and in aid of souls. To these corporal favors He has added many spiritual ones through His infinite goodness, among which I count as great His having called and wanted me in the Company at the age of fourteen, and, by His most liberal grace and to my great confusion in corresponding to it, having preserved me from every mortal sin, and given me grace and a living desire not only to offer myself to Him in Religion with the holy vows, but also to the labors and sufferings even to the last drop of blood, amid the swords and flames of Japan. And therefore I supplicate Your Paternity with all sincerity and affection to ratify the will of God, and to cooperate, so that I may render to my Lord that life which He has preserved to me from so many dangers, that I may sacrifice it to Him in those places and at those times in which persecution rages most cruelly against Christians and preachers of the Gospel, for such is and always shall be my one desire; and while I attend to providing myself with solid virtues and learning for the service of God in those countries, I shall be awaiting, together with His holy blessing, the long-desired grace, as I once more supplicate Your Paternity for the only consolation of your unworthy son. I wish you, at the close, the happiest feasts of Holy Christmas, and I commend myself greatly to your prayers and holy sacrifices."
    ]
   },
   {
    "n": 73,
    "year": 1631,
    "date": "January 12, 1631",
    "place": "Milan",
    "archival": "ARSI, FG 739, 101",
    "excerpt": "A year ago I gave Your Paternity account of the desire which God our Lord communicated to me to spend my life and shed my blood for love of my Lord, and for the salvation of souls, in Japan; therefore, lest it perhaps be thought that in the midst of so many miseries it had been extinguished or cooled, I have wished to renew the request made to you, especially since the desire has been renewed in me, and indeed increased all the more. The tokens of the divine will signified to Your Paternity in my other letter I believe to be so many and so clear that there is no longer room to doubt it; but if any doubt remained, let the miracle worked by the Lord in preserving me from death in the midst of the poisonous contagion wholly remove it; for having dealt so intimately with a fellow student of mine stricken with the plague two days before his death, assisting him in everything, even to holding his forehead for a great while, while he burned all over with the pestilential fever, I cannot believe that God has reserved my life to me for any other purpose than that I might render it to Him more lavishly and gloriously amid the flames and butchery of Japan.",
    "kind": "abridged",
    "full_paras": [
     "A year ago I gave Your Paternity account of the desire which God our Lord communicated to me to spend my life and shed my blood for love of my Lord, and for the salvation of souls, in Japan; therefore, lest it perhaps be thought that in the midst of so many miseries it had been extinguished or cooled, I have wished to renew the request made to you, especially since the desire has been renewed in me, and indeed increased all the more. The tokens of the divine will signified to Your Paternity in my other letter I believe to be so many and so clear that there is no longer room to doubt it; but if any doubt remained, let the miracle worked by the Lord in preserving me from death in the midst of the poisonous contagion wholly remove it; for having dealt so intimately with a fellow student of mine stricken with the plague two days before his death, assisting him in everything, even to holding his forehead for a great while, while he burned all over with the pestilential fever, I cannot believe that God has reserved my life to me for any other purpose than that I might render it to Him more lavishly and gloriously amid the flames and butchery of Japan. I shall not extend myself further in signifying to you the ardent longing which Our Lord communicates to me thereof, sufficing to say that I burn and am consumed; and holding it for certain that the same God who gives it to me will also manifest it to the heart of Your Paternity, I close, humbly asking your holy blessing."
    ]
   },
   {
    "n": 74,
    "year": 1631,
    "date": "March 2, 1631",
    "place": "Milan",
    "archival": "ARSI, FG 739, 108",
    "excerpt": "I shall seem troublesome to Your Paternity for so often recalling to you the ardent longing communicated to me by the Lord to shed my blood in Japan for His love and for the salvation of souls. It is true, Father; I confess it; it is little since I wrote to you, but I can no longer bear it; from the last letter I wrote you until now I have restrained myself, not to weary you with my letters, but now the desire presses too hard, and I can wait no more; therefore I have wished with this one to lay open my heart to you, so that, should occasion offer itself to dispatch people to those parts, I may not be among the last to be sent.",
    "kind": "abridged",
    "full_paras": [
     "I shall seem troublesome to Your Paternity for so often recalling to you the ardent longing communicated to me by the Lord to shed my blood in Japan for His love and for the salvation of souls. It is true, Father; I confess it; it is little since I wrote to you, but I can no longer bear it; from the last letter I wrote you until now I have restrained myself, not to weary you with my letters, but now the desire presses too hard, and I can wait no more; therefore I have wished with this one to lay open my heart to you, so that, should occasion offer itself to dispatch people to those parts, I may not be among the last to be sent. Everything seems nothing to me, and there is nothing that fully satisfies me, until the happy lot of going to Japan falls to me. Every hour, in every place, and in whatever action, I remember most loving Japan; even in sleep itself it often seems to me that I am placed on the cross, awaiting the final lance wound from the executioner, and so great is the sadness I feel afterward, seeing it all to be vain, as was great before the joy which so long-desired an end gave me: in short, I think of nothing but Japan, and all that is not Japan seems nothing to me. So then, the indications of the divine will being so manifest, as Your Paternity may infer from my other letters, with the addition of the ardent longing which is increased in me daily by the same Lord, I pray Your Paternity not to despise the prayers of your unworthy son, but rather to ratify the divine will; and I meanwhile, daily supplicating His Divine Majesty for the fulfillment of this my desire, shall be awaiting, together with your holy blessing, the long-desired grace; and at the close I commend myself with all my heart to your holy sacrifices and prayers."
    ]
   },
   {
    "n": 77,
    "year": 1633,
    "date": "January 29, 1633",
    "place": "Milan",
    "archival": "ARSI, FG 739, 225",
    "excerpt": "Mindful of your promise to me, as benign as it was liberal, made in several letters, concerning the fulfillment of my vocation already several times manifested to you, I have not wished to fail to renew the memory of it in you, especially since it seems to me that now the occasion offers itself to complete the work, through the offering made, as I have understood, by Your Paternity to the Supreme Pontiff of a hundred laborers to cultivate those vineyards; therefore I pray you, prostrate at your feet, to be pleased to console me this time, especially since it seems to me that this is also a fitting time for me, finding myself in the last year of philosophy, so that it would be no disturbance at all to my studies, and I could be more apt to learn, together with the language of the country, whatever else might be imposed upon me.",
    "kind": "abridged",
    "full_paras": [
     "Mindful of your promise to me, as benign as it was liberal, made in several letters, concerning the fulfillment of my vocation already several times manifested to you, I have not wished to fail to renew the memory of it in you, especially since it seems to me that now the occasion offers itself to complete the work, through the offering made, as I have understood, by Your Paternity to the Supreme Pontiff of a hundred laborers to cultivate those vineyards; therefore I pray you, prostrate at your feet, to be pleased to console me this time, especially since it seems to me that this is also a fitting time for me, finding myself in the last year of philosophy, so that it would be no disturbance at all to my studies, and I could be more apt to learn, together with the language of the country, whatever else might be imposed upon me. I pray Your Paternity therefore to be pleased to hear my words and my enkindled desire, without regard to the imperfections of this your unworthy son, assuring you that now I shall begin a new life, with a new religious spirit, so that the Lord God may deign to grant me the desired grace; for which I once more supplicate Your Paternity, as my only consolation; and at the close I pray you for your holy blessing."
    ]
   },
   {
    "n": 78,
    "year": 1633,
    "date": "May 12, 1633",
    "place": "Milan",
    "archival": "ARSI, FG 739, 238",
    "excerpt": "It seems to me now that the time has come to effectuate my so many times manifested desire to be once sent to the country so longed for by me, of the Indies. I shall not repeat a long enumeration concerning the beginning, progress, and end, the motives and signs of the Divine will concerning this mission, supposing it all manifest to Your Paternity by means of my other letters; only I pray you, since the signs of the Divine vocation seem so manifest, not to fail to fulfill what the Lord God designs to do through me, with the addition also to this of the goodwill shown me by the Father Procurator of those parts.",
    "kind": "abridged",
    "full_paras": [
     "It seems to me now that the time has come to effectuate my so many times manifested desire to be once sent to the country so longed for by me, of the Indies. I shall not repeat a long enumeration concerning the beginning, progress, and end, the motives and signs of the Divine will concerning this mission, supposing it all manifest to Your Paternity by means of my other letters; only I pray you, since the signs of the Divine vocation seem so manifest, not to fail to fulfill what the Lord God designs to do through me, with the addition also to this of the goodwill shown me by the Father Procurator of those parts. Of your grace, do not deprive this unworthy son of yours of so great a consolation; which while I am awaiting, I shall endeavor to make myself a more worthy subject of that mission, asking you therefore for your holy blessing."
    ]
   }
  ]
 },
